{"name":"Yellow Jasmine","hex":"#eee8aa","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"yellow jasmine","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Chrysojasminum humile (syn. Jasminum humile), native to Asia","Any of several species of plants with fragrant flowers resembling Jasminum humile\nGelsemium sempervirens, native to tropical and warm temperate regions of the Americas\nPittosporum revolutum, native to Australia"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/yellow_jasmine"}],"entries":[{"title":"Yellow jasmine","blocks":[{"text":"Yellow jasmine or wild yellow jasmine is a common name for several plants and may refer to:"},{"items":["Gelsemium sempervirens, native to tropical and warm temperate regions of the Americas","Jasminum humile, native to Asia and naturalized in Europe","Jasminum mesnyi, native to Vietnam and southern China and naturalized in North America","Pittosporum revolutum, native to Australia"]}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yellow_jasmine"},{"title":"Jasmine","blocks":[{"text":"Jasmine is a genus of shrubs and vines in the olive family of Oleaceae. It contains around 200 species native to tropical and warm temperate regions of Asia, Africa, and Oceania. Jasmines are widely cultivated for the characteristic fragrance of their flowers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Common_Jasmine.jpg/330px-Common_Jasmine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jasmine"},{"title":"Yellow","blocks":[{"text":"Yellow is a color between green and orange on the spectrum of light. It is evoked by light with a dominant wavelength of roughly 575–585 nm. It is a primary color in subtractive color systems, used in painting or color printing. In the RGB color model, used to create colors on television and computer screens, yellow is a secondary color made by combining red and green at equal intensity. Carotenoids give the characteristic yellow color to autumn leaves, corn, canaries, daffodils, and lemons, as well as egg yolks, buttercups, and bananas. They absorb light energy and protect plants from photo damage in some cases. Sunlight has a slight yellowish hue when the Sun is near the horizon, due to atmospheric scattering of shorter wavelengths."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg/330px-Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yellow"},{"title":"Jasmine (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The color jasmine is a pale tint of yellow, displayed at right. It is a representation of the average color of the more yellowish lower part of the pale yellowish white colored jasmine flower. The first recorded use of jasmine as a color name in English was in 1925."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Jsambac.jpg/330px-Jsambac.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jasmine_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Jasmine_.jpg/500px-Jasmine_.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Jasmine_.jpg/1280px-Jasmine_.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Jasmine_.jpg/500px-Jasmine_.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Common jasmine","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJasmine_.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Jasmine_Rose_photo.jpg/500px-Jasmine_Rose_photo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Jasmine_Rose_photo.jpg/1280px-Jasmine_Rose_photo.jpg?utm_source=